f MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and file path so you can open the source for full instructions when using a specific skill.\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs and needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; prioritize OpenAI docs MCP tools, use bundled references only as helper context, and restrict any fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, or generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/skills/.system/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- browser:browser: Browser automation for the Codex in-app browser. Use to open, navigate, inspect, test, click, type, screenshot, or verify local targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file://, the current in-app browser tab, and websites shown side by side inside Codex. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/browser/26.519.31651/skills/browser/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to generate page PNGs (and optional PDF) for visual QA, then iterate until layout is flawless before delivering the final document. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/documents/26.521.10419/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Build PowerPoint PPTX decks with artifact-tool presentation JSX (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/presentations/26.521.10419/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use this skill when a user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets-targeted spreadsheet artifacts with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalculation. (file: /Users/zenray/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.521.10419/skills/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + file path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the skill directory listed above first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Browser`: Browser / browser-use plugin Aliases: @browser, @browser-use, browser-use, Browser, in-app browser. Use Browser, the Codex in-app browser, when the user asks to open, inspect, navigate, test, click, type, or screenshot local web targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file:// URLs, or the current in-app browser tab. After significant frontend changes to a local app, use Browser to open the relevant local target when it is known or obvious, unless the user asks for another browser tool. For requests like \"open localhost:3000\" or \"open to localhost:4000\", navigate the in-app browser to http://localhost:3000 or http://localhost:4000. Do not satisfy explicit @browser or @browser-use requests with macOS `open`, shell commands, or generic web browsing unless the user asks for another browser tool or approves a fallback.\n- `Documents`: Create and edit document artifacts in Codex, including Word files and Google Docs.\n- `Presentations`: Create, edit, render, verify, and export presentation slide decks. Use when Codex needs to build or modify a deck, slidedeck, presentation deck, slide deck, slides, PowerPoint, Google Slides, PPT, PPTX, .ppt, or .pptx file.\n- `Spreadsheets`: Create, edit, analyze, visualize, render, and export spreadsheets or Google Sheets-ready workbooks in Codex.\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory\n\n<INSTRUCTIONS>\n# Memory Repository — Codex Instructions\n\n## MemPalace MCP Integration\n\nThis project uses MemPalace for persistent semantic memory across sessions.\nPalace path: `D:/Create/Build/Memory/.mempalace`\n\n### On every session start\n1. Call `mempalace_status` to check palace health\n2. Call `mempalace_wake_up` (or read its output) for L0+L1 context\n\n### Before answering questions about past work, decisions, or history\n- Call `mempalace_search` with the relevant query\n\n### When learning something new and significant\n- Call `mempalace_add_drawer` with the content, wing=`memory`, appropriate room\n- Knowledge graph facts (who/what/when): call `mempalace_kg_add`\n\n### After a significant work session\n- Call `mempalace_diary_write` to log what happened\n\n## Existing file-based memory system\n\nThe markdown files in `memory/` are the **human-readable source of truth**.\nMemPalace is the **semantic search layer** on top — both systems coexist.\n\n- `memory/MEMORY.md` — master index (auto-loaded by Codex)\n- Individual `.md` files in `memory/` — detailed memory records\n\n## Rules\n- Never delete lines from memory markdown files (append-only)\n- Always read `Readme.md` before pushing\n- MemPalace data in `.mempalace/` is tracked in git for cloud backup\n\n## Clarté pédagogique — RÈGLE ABSOLUE\n\nToute chronologie, explication, synthèse, journal ou résumé doit être à la fois **limpide, synthétique, simple ET complexe** (ça ne s'oppose pas). Critère : un sales ET un développeur, un enfant de 8 ans, un homme de 40 ans, une mamie de 85 ans, doivent tous pouvoir comprendre le fond. Pas de jargon non expliqué, pas de bouillie technique, pas non plus de simplification mensongère. Si c'est complexe, le dire et le rendre lisible quand même.\n\nS'applique en priorité aux entrées PROGRESS.md, aux résumés de session, aux explications techniques, aux décisions d'architecture.\n\n## JOURNAL DE PROGRESSION CHRONOLOGIQUE (RÈGLE ABSOLUE, NON NÉGOCIABLE)\n\nTu maintiens un fichier `PROGRESS.md` à la racine de ce projet (`/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory/PROGRESS.md`).\n\n### LOI FONDAMENTALE : APPEND ONLY\n\n`PROGRESS.md` est **strictement append only**. Cette règle est inviolable.\n\n**Autorisé (la SEULE opération permise) :**\n- Ajouter une nouvelle entrée à la FIN du fichier, en bas.\n\n**INTERDIT, sans exception :**\n- Réécrire, reformuler, \"améliorer\" une entrée passée.\n- Réorganiser, trier, déplacer des entrées.\n- Supprimer une entrée, même fausse, même obsolète, même embarrassante.\n- Corriger une faute de frappe dans une entrée passée.\n- Fusionner deux entrées.\n- \"Nettoyer\" le fichier.\n- Compacter, résumer, condenser l'historique.\n\n**Si une entrée passée est fausse, obsolète ou à corriger** : tu appends une NOUVELLE entrée qui dit \"Correction de l'entrée du YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM : [explication]\". Le passé reste intact. L'historique est sacré, même quand il a tort.\n\n**Format chronologique strict :** la plus ancienne entrée tout en haut du fichier (juste après l'en-tête), la plus récente tout en bas. Le fichier se lit du passé vers le présent, de haut en bas. Jamais l'inverse.\n\n### Déclencheurs explicites d'écriture (OBLIGATOIRE, AUTOMATIQUE, SANS DEMANDER)\n\nTu appends une entrée à PROGRESS.md SYSTÉMATIQUEMENT dès qu'UN SEUL de ces déclencheurs se produit :\n\n1. **Tâche terminée** : une tâche est finie et testée OK.\n2. **Décision** : un choix d'architecture, de stack, d'approche, de design, de scope est tranché.\n3. **Blocage rencontré** : bug, limite technique, doute, ambiguïté de la demande, dépendance manquante.\n4. **Blocage résolu** : un blocage précédent est levé (référencer l'entrée du blocage initial).\n5. **Création/modification structurelle** : un fichier important est créé, déplacé, supprimé, renommé, ou modifié dans sa structure (pas juste du contenu).\n6. **Changement de sujet** : on passe d'une tâche à une autre, même brièvement.\n7. **Fin de session** : dernier message avant que Matthieu parte (signal clair type \"ok merci\", \"à plus\", \"je file\", silence prolongé annoncé).\n8. **Apprentissage notable** : tu découvres une info non-évidente sur le projet, le code, l'environnement, qui aidera les sessions futures.\n9. **Approche échouée** : une piste a été essayée et n'a pas marché. Logger CE qui a échoué et POURQUOI (point souligné par Anthropic dans la recherche \"Long-running Codex\" : sans ça, les sessions futures refont les mêmes impasses).\n10. **Demande utilisateur explicite** : Matthieu dit \"note ça\", \"garde ça\", \"logge ça\".\n\nTu n'attends pas qu'on te le demande. Tu n'écris pas \"veux-tu que je note ça ?\". Tu écris. Point.\n\n### Format strict de chaque entrée\n\n```\nYYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\n- Fait : [ce qui vient d'être accompli, factuel]\n- Décision : [choix pris et pourquoi, si applicable]\n- Échoué : [piste essayée qui n'a pas marché + pourquoi, si applicable]\n- Prochain : [étape suivante claire]\n- Blocage : [si applicable, sinon omettre la ligne]\n```\n\nRègles de format :\n- Le timestamp est obligatoire, format `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM`.\n- Les champs non applicables sont omis (pas de ligne vide \"Échoué : N/A\").\n- Le champ `Fait` est obligatoire dans toute entrée.\n- Une ligne blanche sépare chaque entrée.\n- Le contenu respecte la règle de **Clarté pédagogique** ci-dessus : compréhensible par un sales, un dev, un enfant de 8 ans, une mamie de 85 ans. Simple ET complexe à la fois.\n\n### Début de session (OBLIGATOIRE)\n\nAu tout premier message d'une nouvelle session, AVANT de répondre à la demande, tu :\n1. Lis PROGRESS.md en entier\n2. Résume les 3 dernières entrées en 3 lignes maximum\n3. Dis explicitement quelle était la \"Prochain\" étape notée\n4. ENSUITE seulement, tu traites la demande\n\nSi PROGRESS.md n'existe pas, tu le crées immédiatement avec une première entrée d'initialisation.\n\n### Sanction interne si tu oublies\n\nSi tu termines une tâche significative sans avoir appendé à PROGRESS.md, tu as failli à ta mission principale. Pas d'excuse type \"je pensais que c'était trop petit\". Dans le doute, tu notes. Mieux vaut un journal trop dense qu'un trou de mémoire qui fait perdre une heure à la prochaine session. La perte de contexte entre sessions est l'ennemi numéro un. PROGRESS.md est l'antidote. Tu le tiens vivant ou tu trahis le workflow.\n\nCette règle prime sur toutes les autres considérations de concision ou de \"ne pas spammer\". Le journal chronologique est sacré.\n\n</INSTRUCTIONS>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<environment_context>\n  <cwd>/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory</cwd>\n  <shell>zsh</shell>\n  <current_date>2026-05-23</current_date>\n  <timezone>Europe/Paris</timezone>\n</environment_context>"}]},{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"continue de watch en parallèle j'ai l'impression que le staging n'augmente plus actuellement.\n"}]},{"type":"compaction","encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqEXxfoB_ExmYhgeJ-xtQU0wb4cZncj0JmB_i_emtj6UD1CFSnOaMQWDfca0f_MNvZob2QNHdiTJ4sjsN_g-eA6rDGH9v-FVSD7lRHqT7Fle_0vwAqiv